      <p>If you're in the US and have travel insurance through World Nomads, SafetyWing, or a similar provider, that will not work for the NLV. You must get proper resident health insurance.</p>

      <h2>How to Apply for NLV Insurance</h2>
      <p>The process is straightforward:</p>

      <ol style="list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.75rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Research providers:</strong> Get a list from your consulate or contact AARP Global, established private health insurers in Spain directly.</li>
        <li><strong>Request a quote:</strong> Provide your age, nationality (some providers care), health status, and whether you have dependents.</li>
        <li><strong>Review the policy document:</strong> Look for zero copayments, repatriation clause, geographic scope covering Spain, and 12-month minimum validity.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase 12+ months upfront:</strong> Don't just buy 3 months. Insurance must remain valid through your entire visa process.</li>
        <li><strong>Obtain proof of insurance:</strong> Get an official certificate/summary showing all coverage details.</li>
        <li><strong>Include in your application:</strong> Submit this certificate with your visa application to the consulate.</li>
      </ol>

